  12   │ ## Introduction
  13   │ 
  14   │ Orchestration Architect the project will use `literate documents` so that 
  15   │ the project can be re-generated from specifications, and to ensure coherence 
  16   │ and an easy way to test the tools as they are being specified from within the 
  17   │ document itself.
  18   │ 
  19   │ The goal is the have `literate specifications` that are able to
  20   │  generate the implementation of the subject matter. 
  21   │ 
  22   │ The idea behind `literate documents` is that the *document* contains a one line 
  23   │ command, that can be executed by tooling, or invoked by the reader, to bootstrap
  24   │ the self-assembly of the document.
  25   │ 
  26   │ The supplied command should `tail` a document from the last heading
  27   │ (enriched using the tools generated by the `SPEC-000 Literate Specification`).
  28   │ 
  29   │ The functionality provided should *identify* macros and regions,
  30   │ defined by `CONCEPT-001 Abstract Compositional Topology`.
  31   │ and then a *literate implementation* heading should contain a block composed of 
  32   │ concatenated macro tokens. The resolution of these tokens should generate the
  33   │ subject of the document.
  34   │ 
  35   │ ## Scope of the Design 
  36   │ 
  37   │ ### Problem Statement
  38   │ 
  39   │ The challenge of keeping documentation update to date with changing
  40   │ specifications due to the architectural decision to embrace chaos and to
  41   │ facilitate a repeatable transisent structure.
  42   │ 
  43   │ The need to reduce dependence on third-party dependencies has lead to
  44   │ difficulties keep track of the monitoring of different components and systems.
  45   │   
  46   │ Furthermore, the development team size of one (myself), and intricate concepts,
  47   │ have lead to challenges with *cognitive overload*.
  48   │    
  49   │ Though work has been made on designing the project managmeent system naturally
  50   │ cohere,  with the natural transclusion (tasks split by project, naturally hide
  51   │ the tasks not being worked on), and the preorders that arise from the manner in
  52   │ which things are worked on, keeping a temporal structure allowing for cognitive
  53   │ mapping to what it important,  and the ability to capture potential resources,
  54   │ should the need come.
  55   │   
  56   │ However, the benefits of this is under utilised due to the complex domain logic,
  57   │ which represents naturality, and it is best explained as a document.
  58   │   
  59   │ Futhermore, the notes system has shown that more documents is not the answer,
  60   │ it is quality that counts, which `literate documents` facilitates builtin
  61   │ self-acutalisation of potential resources.
  62   │ 
  63   │ ### Goals and Objectives
  64   │ 
  65   │ Since the system has been designed to be able to assemble itself from
  66   │ specifications, it just makes sense to embrace Knuth's literate writing 
  67   │ concepts.
  68   │ 
  69   │ As work has already been done in making a set of primitives that is able to
  70   │ compose operations, and match regions it will allow for these category theoretic
  71   │ concepts to demonstrate its generality.
  72   │ 
  73   │ The challenges that need to be addressed:
  74   │ 
  75   │ - A consistent description of the types of documents;
  76   │ 
  77   │ - Markers that can identify tokens from the implicit structure,
  78   │     such its specifications facilitations simple extension
  79   │ 
  80   │ - A consistent URI schema and resolution 
  81   │ 
  82   │ - Configurations have a standard location,
  83   │   that is decoupled from the environment
  84   │ 
  85   │ - The need for the shell configurations (zsh, bash and fish) to be configured
  86   │     uniformly such that:
  87   │ 
  88   │     - Required *OA Environment Paths* can be added.
  89   │ 
  90   │     - Environment Variables are available 
  91   │     
  92   │ - The system needs to be able to generate the necessary components across
  93   │   various environments 
